Yes—and there’s an extraordinary blind spot in our picture of prehistoric Earth: we don’t just have missing creatures. We have entire missing ecosystems.

Your jellyfish example is almost perfect, with one surprising exception: scientists have found jellyfish fossils more than 500 million years old, including rare Cambrian specimens. But soft-bodied creatures are far less likely to fossilize than animals with shells, teeth, or bones.

And the modern ocean shows how big the problem is. According to NOAA’s 2026 figures, only 28.7% of the seafloor has been mapped using modern high-resolution techniques, and humans have visually observed less than 0.001% of the deep seafloor. Mapping the bottom is different from discovering the organisms living there.

That suggests a third row for our poster: The Hidden Worlds—eight additional prehistoric and modern habitats revealing the extraordinary creatures our usual history overlooks.

This raises a deeper problem for our History Stereoscope: the fossil record is not a census of ancient life. It’s a heavily filtered sample.

An organism had to die under conditions favorable to preservation, survive geological processes, become exposed or accessible, be discovered, and be correctly interpreted. And those filters aren’t random. Soft tissue often decays first, potentially removing the anatomical features scientists need to classify the organism.

There’s an additional twist: much ancient oceanic crust and its sediments have been recycled through subduction. Some prehistoric ocean floors are physically gone, although marine fossils also survive in sediments that later became land.

For our Museum, I’d introduce a Ghost Worlds lens: a scientifically constrained view of life that might be missing from the record, with its uncertainty plainly displayed.

That gives us three distinct maps: what actually existed (ultimately unknowable in full), what could be preserved, and what humans have discovered.

And the gaps between those maps may be as fascinating as the fossils themselves.

We haven’t necessarily discovered the strangest creatures Earth ever produced. We’ve discovered some of the strangest creatures that happened to leave evidence we could recover.